Case Study: Banking is one of the most familiar real-life database applications. A bank must keep a record of its customers and their accounts, make sure every account balance stays consistent, and log each deposit and withdrawal the moment it happens. This case study models a simplified retail banking system, in which a customer holds one or more accounts and every movement of money is recorded as a transaction, and shows how a desktop front end can carry out these operations directly against the database. Entities and Relationships: The design is built from three entities. Customer (custid, cname, phone) represents the account holder. Account (accno, custid, balance) belongs to exactly one customer through a foreign key on custid, and its balance is constrained never to fall below zero. Transactions (txnid, accno, txntype, amount, balance_after, txntime) records every operation performed on an account, whether OPEN, DEPOSIT or WITHDRAW, and is linked to Account by a foreign key. The relationships are therefore one Customer to many Accounts, and one Account to many Transactions.  Fig. 12.1: ER diagram (customer 1:N account 1:N transactions). The tables are in Third Normal Form: customer details are stored once, and each transaction refers to its account only through accno. transactions.balance_after is a deliberate snapshot of the balance, so a statement can be shown without replaying the ledger. Features: The application supports six operations: Insert (open a new account together with its customer), Update (edit a customer's name and phone), Delete (close an account and remove its transaction history), Deposit and Withdraw (each guarded by an insufficient-balance check), and History (view the transaction log of a single account or of all accounts). A balance can change only through Deposit or Withdraw, so the account balance and the transaction ledger always stay in stepDatabase Connectivity: The front end is built with Tkinter, Python's standard GUI toolkit, and connects to the SQLite database through Python's built-in sqlite3 module. Every button on the window issues SQL over this connection: for example, Deposit runs an UPDATE on account and then an INSERT into transactions within a single database transaction, so both writes succeed together or neither is applied. Referential integrity is switched on with PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON, and table-level rules such as CHECK (balance >= 0) are enforced by the database engine itself, not by the interface. The schema and the complete program follow. Architecture: BankApp (Tkinter) is the presentation layer and only calls BankDB methods; BankDB is the application layer that validates input and controls transactions; the sqlite3 driver (the role JDBC or ODBC plays in other stacks) carries SQL to the SQLite DBMS. Here all layers run on one computer; a real bank would put an application server between the app and the database, reached over HTTPS.   Fig. 12.2: Layers and connectivity path from the GUI to the database. Schema (schema.sql): CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customer (     custid INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,     cname  TEXT NOT NULL,     phone  TEXT ); CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS account (     accno   INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,     custid  INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES customer(custid),     balance REAL    NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (balance >= 0) );  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS transactions (     txnid         INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,     accno         INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES account(accno),     txntype       TEXT    NOT NULL CHECK (txntype IN ('OPEN', 'DEPOSIT', 'WITHDRAW')),     amount        REAL    NOT NULL CHECK (amount >= 0),     balance_after REAL    NOT NULL,     txntime       TEXT    NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now', 'localtime')) ); Program (bank_app.py): import os import sqlite3 import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk, messagebox, simpledialog  BASE_DIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)) DB_FILE = os.path.join(BASE_DIR, "bank.db") SCHEMA_FILE =